# XCU iconv
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Codeset names are implementation-defined and iconv -l lists them in an
# unspecified format. The tests take every word of that list that has no
# slash as a candidate codeset name and use the first one that converts a
# plain text from itself to itself; the checks that follow rest on that
# identity conversion. Charmap conversions are not used (they need a charmap
# file with every portable character, and are optional).

assertion  XCU.iconv.001
anchor     utilities/iconv.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/iconv.html#STDOUT
class      required
says       iconv -l exits with status zero and writes the supported codeset
           names to standard output.
test       t/001-list.sh

assertion  XCU.iconv.002
anchor     utilities/iconv.html#STDOUT
anchor     utilities/iconv.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       Converting a text from a supported codeset to the same codeset
           writes exactly the text, and nothing else, to standard output.
test       t/002-identity.sh

assertion  XCU.iconv.003
anchor     utilities/iconv.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/iconv.html#STDIN
class      required
says       Several file operands are converted in order and the output is
           their concatenation; a file operand of - stands for standard input
           at that position.
test       t/003-file-operands.sh

assertion  XCU.iconv.004
anchor     utilities/iconv.html#EXIT STATUS
anchor     utilities/iconv.html#OPERANDS
class      required
says       A file operand that cannot be opened gives a status above zero.
test       t/004-missing-file.sh

assertion  XCU.iconv.005
anchor     utilities/iconv.html#OPTIONS
class      required
says       Adding -c or -s does not change the exit status, and -s keeps
           messages about invalid characters off standard error; the same
           input run with none of them, -c, -s and -cs gives one status.
test       t/005-c-and-s.sh

assertion  XCU.iconv.006
anchor     utilities/iconv.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/iconv.html#DESCRIPTION
class      untestable
says       With charmap files for -f and -t, the conversion joins the two
           charmaps on their symbolic names; -c omits characters invalid in
           the input codeset.
reason     A charmap file must name every portable character and give an
           encoding to each, the codeset of a charmap-less -f or -t is
           implementation-defined, and the result for characters with no
           counterpart is documented by the implementation, not specified.
